@nickregeda It seems the issue persists. The `equals` function is focusing on the returned value from the computation callback rather than the original signal. I tested a different approach, and it worked as expected.

### TS Code
```
import { CommonModule } from '@angular/common';
import { Component, computed, linkedSignal, signal } from '@angular/core';

interface IUser {id: number, name: string}
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  imports: [CommonModule],
  styleUrl: './app.component.scss'
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = 'v-19';
  activeUser = signal<any>({id: 123, name: 'Morgan'});
  email = linkedSignal(() => ({email:`${this.activeUser().name}@example.com`, ...this.activeUser()}), {
    equal: (a, b) => a.id == b.id,
  });

  alternateEmail = linkedSignal({
    source: this.activeUser,
    computation: user => ({email:`${user.name}@example.com`, ...user}),
    equal: (a, b) => a.id === b.id,
  });

  constructor() {
    setTimeout(() => {
    this.activeUser.set({id: 1234, name: 'Morgan123', isAmin: false});
    }, 3000);
  }
}
```
### HTML Code
```

<p>{{alternateEmail().email}}</p>
<p>{{email().email}}</p>
```

In the example above, instead of returning a string value from the computation, I returned a full object with the required property added. This resolved the issue, and it started working as expected.

It seems the behavior described in the documentation differs from how it actually works.

Uncertain About the Expected Behavior of This Feature

The equal function is used to determine if the signal itself (in the case the `linkedSignal`) was changed. The dirtyness of the source signal is determined by that said signal. 
The example should probably be 

```ts
const activeUser = signal({id: 123, name: 'Morgan', isAdmin: true});
const email = linkedSignal(() => ({id:`${activeUser().name}@example.com`}), {
  // Consider the user as the same if it's the same `id`.
  equal: (a, b) => a.id === b.id,
});
// Or, if separating `source` and `computation`
const alternateEmail = linkedSignal({
  source: activeUser,
  computation: user => ({id:`${user.name}@example.com`}),
  equal: (a, b) => a.id === b.id,
});
// This update to `activeUser` does not cause `email` or `alternateEmail`
// to update because the `id` is the same.
activeUser.set({id: 123, name: 'Morgan', isAdmin: false});
```
